                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Next on the list of the random short horror game extravaganza.\n\nI've unlocked 2/5 endings and the impression this game has left me so far is \"unremarkable\". The story is not really anything new, a guy in a coma is stuck in a nightmare world, and at least in the 2 endings I have gotten so far, and the unlockable notes I have found during that time, it doesn't really go much further than that either.\n\nGameplay is mostly just puzzles, and they oftentimes commit what I consider a sin for puzzles, where they are trial and error based rather than logic based. For example one of the earliest puzzles is a maze you can construct with some dials that move pieces of the maze across a column or row. Hidden in that maze is an item you need to progress. Where is it? Well the game never tells you, outside \"the solution is in the wall.\" So.... you then have to use trial and error to run back and forth between the dials and the maze as you search 12 different parts of the maze for the right wall. Lame. \n\nThe rest of the game is an object hunt, looking around maze like levels clicking doors to find which ones open and which ones don't, searching for items that help your progress the level. The item usage itself, thankfully makes sense for the most part. What doesn't make sense is certain items appearing based off certain scripted triggers. In a certain part of the game I mentally marked off an area of the level as \"clear\", not realizing that items would spawn there AFTER doing things elsewhere in the map and spent a good amount of time scouring the rest of the map before I decided to return back to the area I noted as clear, only to find there was an item now present there, but not only that, picking up that item then further spawns another item hidden elsewhere in that building. Once I can look past, but twice in a row and you're just testing my patience.\n\nThere is also a few scripted sequences where you need to run away from something, and they are incredibly one note, straightforward and easy. It mixes it up by making you solve a puzzle during one of the sequences, which basically just amounts to running circles until you have pieced it together, or in other words, making it unnecessarily annoying rather than adding complexity or depth to the puzzle.\n\nI will give props to the general art direction however, the monochromatic color scheme with the occasional splash of red really is striking and pretty unique. It's what kept me moving through the game the most, just seeing what kind of environments could be designed with the color scheme in mind. Unfortunately it kind of unravels some when you start to see the same repeating set pieces over and over and over again. Especially when those particular set pieces are used for shock value, the value of which quickly wears off through repetition. Then there are cheap jumpscares hidden throughout the levels, and they are so low effort that it is literally the same jumpscare everytime and only managed to get me because of the surprise factor of a loud noise and quick image playing on screen, than any kind of unsettling imagery or complex scare or clever use of atmosphere.\n\nThe whole game kind of just feels like big case of missed potential. The framework for something cool and unique is here, but somewhere early on the cool ideas stopped and what's leftover is a repetitious game that feels underbaked.\n\nI don't think I will be making an effort to unlock the other endings. I read there are a few more levels associated with the final endings, but I just haven't been given enough to drive me to seek that out.",
